  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cool air mass in place is bringing frost to \nparts of the region early this morning, particularly across the \nPoconos and northwestern New Jersey.\n\nCanadian high pressure has brought a cool airmass to the area. The\nhigh is gradually beginning to slide offshore this morning. \nThis setup has lead to very good radiational cooling conditions\nearly this morning due to the mainly clear sky and near calm\nwind under the center of the high. For these reasons our \nforecast continues to undercut NBM temps for most areas with the\nmention of patchy frost in the normally cooler areas including \ninterior southern NJ. Farther north, frost is more extensive so\nwe continue with the Frost Advisory for early this morning for \nour Pocono zones and Sussex County NJ. These area have the best \nchances for temperatures radiating to freezing, and in fact KMPO\nreached freezing already, marking the end of the growing season\nat least in the coldest portions of our region. Whether that\ncauses us to declare any entire forecast zones as having ended\nthe growing season remains to be seen, as it generally needs to\nbe at least half the zone and that part is not yet clear. Some \ncolder locations farther south, particularly across the NJ Pine \nBarrens, are also making a run at 32 early this morning, but we\nhaven't seen any other major locations reach there just yet.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tranquil weather will prevail this week \ncontinuing into the weekend with no significant weather hazards \nanticipated. \n\nHigh pressure will be in control through most of the week \nresulting in mostly clear/sunny skies. Following this morning's\nchilly weather temperatures will moderate later today through \nThursday as the flow turns southwesterly ahead of the next cold \nfront. In fact highs by Thursday afternoon look to reach the \nmiddle to upper 70s for most of the area, perhaps even touching\n80, though latest NBM has nudged the temps down a bit. The cold\nfront moves through by late Thursday as a dry front and will be\nfollowed by high pressure re- establishing itself over the area\nfor Friday through at least Saturday with seasonable \ntemperatures. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...The next system will approach the area later this \nweekend bringing chances for rain as early as Sunday afternoon.\n\nHigh pressure shifts offshore by Sunday as the next low \npressure system (what is now TD 9) approaches from the south \nand west. There are some model differences regarding this system\nand its arrival time in the area. The latest ECMWF slowed down a\nbit compared to its earlier run, while the GFS has sped up. In \nany event, the system should be fairly progressive but will \nlikely have significant moisture with it (PWATs potentially \nreaching a rather tropical 2 inches) so the rain could be heavy \nat times with the potential for some areas to see 1-2 inches.",
